    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "King Lake began as an oxbow lake created when the Elkhorn River changed course after a flood in the late 1880s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/king_lake_nebraska"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1925, heirs of early settler George King sold the land to New York developer Arthur Smadbeck, sparking the opening of a weekend resort for Omaha residents.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/king_lake_nebraska"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "King Lake has endured severe floods in 1960, 2010, and 2019, each time submerging homes and roads.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/king_lake_nebraska"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "About 94% of King Lake homes face flood risk in the next three decades, reflecting its low‑lying floodplain.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
